  "docTitle": "In-Memory File System - SDE Prep Dojo",
  "description": "Design an in-memory file system (Uber's round): a Composite tree, path walking with wildcards, a find built from composable Specification filters, and a read-write lock, in Java, Python and C++14.",
  "hero": {
    "tag": "header",
    "attrs": {
      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\">\n    <span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"3\">Level 3 - Intermediate</span>\n    <span class=\"time\">Read 60 min</span><span class=\"chip\">Build 90 min</span>\n    <span class=\"chip\">Machine-coding case study</span>\n    <span class=\"chip\">Rank 18 of 45</span>\n  </div>\n  <h1>In-Memory File System</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Build the Unix directory tree in memory: <code>mkdir</code>, <code>cd</code>, <code>pwd</code>, <code>ls</code>, files, <code>rm -r</code>, a <code>cd</code> whose <code>*</code> can mean \".\", \"..\" or any child (the Uber twist), and a Unix <code>find</code> with combinable filters. You will see why it is a Composite, why filters are Specifications, and how the whole thing is a tiny tmpfs sitting on the same ideas as the Linux VFS.</p>\n"
  },
